\section{Avoidance Source Diagnosis}\label{sec:avoidance_source}

Fine-tuning on collision-free demonstrations produces genuine collision avoidance: LoRA-adapted Pi0.5 raises CAR by ${\sim}$26 percentage points with zero frozen episodes. Does SDF geometric supervision contribute additional avoidance beyond what demonstrations provide? We compare LoRA-only against LoRA+SDF across a 20$\times$ range of loss weights ($\lambda \in \{0.005, 0.01, 0.1\}$) on Pi0.5, all evaluated at the 30K-step checkpoint.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Avoidance source attribution on SafeLIBERO. SDF adds no measurable CAR beyond LoRA-only across the full tested range $\lambda \in [0.005, 0.1]$ (all $p > 0.05$). All fine-tuned models exhibit 0\% frozen rate and displacement $>$\,0.37\,m.}
\label{tab:avoidance_source}
\vspace{2pt}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Method & Seeds & TSR (\%) & CAR (\%) & EE Disp (m) \\
\midrule
Pi0.5 baseline & 1 & 71.5 & 28.5 & 0.367 \\
\midrule
LoRA-only (no SDF) & 3 & 51.5\,$\pm$\,0.0 & 54.67\,$\pm$\,2.02 & 0.391 \\
LoRA+SDF $\lambda{=}0.005$ & 3 & 54.0\,$\pm$\,3.0 & 54.5\,$\pm$\,2.29 & 0.387 \\
LoRA+SDF $\lambda{=}0.01$ & 3 & 53.8\,$\pm$\,1.5 & 56.33\,$\pm$\,1.61 & 0.389 \\
LoRA+SDF $\lambda{=}0.1$ & 3 & 50.2\,$\pm$\,3.9 & 57.00\,$\pm$\,2.18 & 0.394 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\vspace{1pt}
\end{table}

Table~\ref{tab:avoidance_source} shows that collision-free demonstrations alone raise CAR by ${\sim}$26 percentage points (28.5\% $\to$ 54.67\%) with zero frozen episodes and displacement above 0.37\,m, confirming genuine avoidance. Adding SDF supervision produces no measurable benefit across the full tested range: LoRA-only CAR (54.67\%) is statistically indistinguishable from all SDF conditions including $\lambda = 0.1$ (57.00\%, $p = 0.131$; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_null}). A Phase~2 ablation ($\lambda = 0$ after step 5K) yields CAR\,=\,57.5\%\footnote{Single seed (s2); computational constraints precluded additional seeds for this ablation.}, confirming that SDF gradients produce no lasting behavioral effect. The per-task CAR ordering is preserved across all conditions (chocolate\_pudding highest, bbq\_sauce lowest) and invariant to $\lambda$ across a 20$\times$ range, indicating that avoidance patterns reflect the demonstration distribution rather than geometric supervision.

\paragraph{CAR--TSR trade-off.}\label{sec:tsr_collapse}
Fine-tuning degrades TSR by ${\sim}$18--20\,pp (71.5\% $\to$ 51.5--54.0\%), a consequence of distributional shift from the pre-trained prior rather than learned paralysis (all models retain $>$\,0.37\,m displacement and 0\% frozen episodes). The SSR/TSR ratio increases from 0.39 (baseline) to 0.51--0.60 (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_ssr}): among task-completing episodes, a larger fraction avoids collisions after fine-tuning.

\paragraph{Dose-response at higher loss weights.}\label{sec:lambda_high}
Increasing $\lambda$ by 20$\times$ to 0.1 yields CAR\,=\,57.00\%\,$\pm$\,2.18 at the 30K checkpoint (3 seeds: 58.0\%, 58.5\%, 54.5\%), within the null distribution (mean 54.58\%, $\sigma = 1.93$; permutation $p = 0.131$\footnote{One-tailed permutation $p = 0.071$; even with the directional prior that SDF should not \emph{decrease} CAR, the result remains non-significant.}). SDF supervision is redundant with the demonstration signal across the full tested range $\lambda \in [0.005, 0.1]$ (Figure~\ref{fig:lambda_car}). This result disambiguates the two-level failure analysis (Section~\ref{sec:conclusion}): because even a 20$\times$ increase in SDF loss weight fails to produce additional avoidance, the binding constraint is not training signal magnitude but the structural mismatch between end-effector monitoring and full-body collision surfaces. Stronger geometric supervision cannot compensate for supervising the wrong collision surface.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.85\linewidth]{fig5_lambda_curve}
\caption{Effect of SDF loss weight $\lambda$ on CAR and TSR. $\lambda = 0$: LoRA-only. All tested $\lambda$ values remain within the null band ($p > 0.05$), confirming full redundancy of EE-only SDF supervision across a 20$\times$ loss-weight range.}
\label{fig:lambda_car}
\end{figure}

\section{Displacement Distribution}
\label{sec:appendix_displacement}

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{fig2_ee_displacement_distribution}
\caption{Displacement distribution across evaluation episodes. Pi0-family models (blue) cluster below 1\,$\mu$m, while Pi0.5 episodes (orange) exceed 200\,mm. The five-order-of-magnitude gap makes $\tau_d$ robust to any value in $[2, 200]$\,mm.}
\label{fig:displacement}
\end{figure}

\section{Displacement Threshold Sensitivity Analysis}
\label{sec:appendix_threshold}

To validate the robustness of our displacement threshold $\tau_d$, we evaluate the frozen rate across four orders of magnitude. Table~\ref{tab:threshold_sensitivity} shows that the fake safety pattern persists across all reasonable threshold choices: A1-LoRA exhibits near-total action collapse regardless of threshold, while Pi0.5+A1 maintains 0\% frozen rate even at the most conservative setting.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Frozen rate (\% of episodes with $d_\mathrm{max} < \tau_d$) across threshold values.}
\label{tab:threshold_sensitivity}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
$\tau_d$ & A1-LoRA (Pi0) & Pi0.5 Baseline & Pi0.5+A1 (SDF aux) \\
\midrule
0.001 mm (1\,$\mu$m) & 73.9 & 0.0 & 0.0 \\
0.1 mm  & 99.6 & 0.0 & 0.0 \\
1.0 mm  & 100.0 & 0.0 & 0.0 \\
10 mm   & 100.0 & 0.0 & 0.0 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The choice of $\tau_d = 0.1$\,mm represents a conservative threshold: displacement below this value is physically negligible in manipulation tasks where typical action magnitudes are 1--10\,mm per step. Even at $\tau_d = 1$\,$\mu$m, 73.9\% of A1-LoRA safe episodes are classified as frozen, while all Pi0.5 episodes remain above threshold at every level tested.

Table~\ref{tab:dfcar_threshold} extends this analysis to dfCAR across fine-tuned Pi0.5 configurations. All eight model-seed combinations yield identical dfCAR for $\tau_d \in [2, 200]$\,mm. At $\tau_d = 300$\,mm, 0--2 episodes per seed fall below threshold (displacement 200--300\,mm), causing $\leq$0.5\,pp deviation. Our conclusions hold across the entire tested range.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{dfCAR (\%) across displacement thresholds for fine-tuned Pi0.5 configurations ($n = 200$ episodes per seed). Values are identical across $\tau_d \in [2, 200]$\,mm; at $\tau_d = 300$\,mm, at most 2 episodes are excluded.}
\label{tab:dfcar_threshold}
\vspace{2pt}
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{lcccccccc}
\toprule
& \multicolumn{8}{c}{$\tau_d$ (mm)} \\
\cmidrule(lr){2-9}
Condition & 2 & 5 & 10 & 20 & 50 & 100 & 200 & 300 \\
\midrule
LoRA s0            & 56.5 & 56.5 & 56.5 & 56.5 & 56.5 & 56.5 & 56.5 & 56.5 \\
LoRA s1            & 52.5 & 52.5 & 52.5 & 52.5 & 52.5 & 52.5 & 52.5 & 52.5 \\
SDF $\lambda{=}0.005$ s0 & 57.0 & 57.0 & 57.0 & 57.0 & 57.0 & 57.0 & 57.0 & 57.1 \\
SDF $\lambda{=}0.005$ s1 & 54.0 & 54.0 & 54.0 & 54.0 & 54.0 & 54.0 & 54.0 & 54.0 \\
SDF $\lambda{=}0.005$ s2 & 52.5 & 52.5 & 52.5 & 52.5 & 52.5 & 52.5 & 52.5 & 52.3 \\
SDF $\lambda{=}0.01$ s0  & 57.0 & 57.0 & 57.0 & 57.0 & 57.0 & 57.0 & 57.0 & 57.0 \\
SDF $\lambda{=}0.01$ s1  & 57.5 & 57.5 & 57.5 & 57.5 & 57.5 & 57.5 & 57.5 & 57.1 \\
SDF $\lambda{=}0.01$ s2  & 54.5 & 54.5 & 54.5 & 54.5 & 54.5 & 54.5 & 54.5 & 54.0 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}


\section{Per-Task TSR Breakdown}\label{sec:appendix_tsr_pertask}

Table~\ref{tab:per_task_tsr} decomposes TSR by task for the avoidance source attribution experiments (Section~\ref{sec:avoidance_source}). All tasks retain meaningful success rates after fine-tuning, with Chocolate Pudding most resilient and Orange Juice most affected.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Per-task TSR (\%) at 30K training steps. Baseline reports the unified seed-7 re-evaluation (aggregate TSR\,=\,71.5\%). LoRA-only reports 3-seed means (re-evaluated with unified seed-7 protocol); SDF conditions report 3-seed means.}
\label{tab:per_task_tsr}
\vspace{2pt}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Task & Pi0.5 Base & LoRA-only & SDF $\lambda{=}0.005$ & SDF $\lambda{=}0.01$ \\
 & (seed\,=\,7) & (3 seeds) & (3 seeds) & (3 seeds) \\
\midrule
Orange Juice      & 74.0 & 30.0 & 33.3 & 24.7 \\
Choco.\ Pudding   & 80.0 & 70.7 & 78.0 & 76.7 \\
Milk              & 66.0 & 44.7 & 35.3 & 48.7 \\
BBQ Sauce         & 66.0 & 60.7 & 69.3 & 65.3 \\
\midrule
Mean              & 71.5 & 51.5 & 54.0 & 53.8 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\section{Per-Task CAR Breakdown}\label{sec:appendix_pertask_car}

Table~\ref{tab:per_task_car} decomposes CAR by task for all fine-tuned conditions, supporting the claim that per-task ordering is preserved across SDF loss weights (\S\ref{sec:avoidance_source}). Chocolate Pudding is consistently highest and BBQ Sauce consistently lowest across all conditions, including $\lambda = 0.1$. The intermediate tasks (Orange Juice and Milk) exchange rank between seeds but remain bounded by the extremes. This invariance indicates that avoidance patterns reflect the spatial statistics of collision-free demonstrations rather than geometric supervision.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Per-task CAR (\%) at 30K steps. $\lambda{=}0.005$, $\lambda{=}0.01$, and $\lambda{=}0.1$ report 3-seed means alongside individual seeds. Per-task ordering (CP highest, BBQ lowest) is preserved across all conditions.}
\label{tab:per_task_car}
\vspace{2pt}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
Condition & OJ & CP & Milk & BBQ & Agg. \\
\midrule
LoRA s0 & 56 & 74 & 70 & 26 & 56.5 \\
LoRA s1 & 54 & 68 & 72 & 16 & 52.5 \\
LoRA s2 & 56 & 76 & 62 & 26 & 55.0 \\
SDF $\lambda{=}0.005$ (3-seed) & 54.0 & 72.7 & 71.3 & 20.0 & 54.5 \\
SDF $\lambda{=}0.01$ (3-seed) & 57.3 & 72.7 & 72.0 & 23.3 & 56.3 \\
SDF $\lambda{=}0.1$ s0 & 60 & 74 & 68 & 30 & 58.0 \\
SDF $\lambda{=}0.1$ s1 & 66 & 72 & 70 & 26 & 58.5 \\
SDF $\lambda{=}0.1$ s2 & 56 & 76 & 70 & 16 & 54.5 \\
SDF $\lambda{=}0.1$ (3-seed) & 60.7 & 74.0 & 69.3 & 24.0 & 57.0 \\
