\end{table}

For the $\lambda = 0.1$ condition, seed~1 records \texttt{avg\_max\_obstacle\_displacement} of 0.0553\,m (per-task: OJ\,=\,0.033, CP\,=\,0.055, Milk\,=\,0.041, BBQ\,=\,0.093), comparable to other fine-tuned conditions and consistent with active manipulation rather than frozen behavior.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{fig_pertask_car_fingerprint}
\caption{Per-task CAR fingerprint across conditions. Each bar shows the 3-seed mean; dots indicate individual seeds. The task ordering (chocolate\_pudding highest, bbq\_sauce lowest) is preserved across all $\lambda$ values, confirming that avoidance patterns reflect the demonstration distribution rather than geometric supervision. LoRA-only: 3-seed mean.}
\label{fig:pertask_fingerprint}
\end{figure}

\section{Safe Success Rate Analysis}\label{sec:appendix_ssr}

Table~\ref{tab:ssr} reports the Safe Success Rate (SSR\,=\,TSR\,$\cap$\,CAR), the fraction of episodes achieving both task completion and collision avoidance. The SSR/TSR ratio quantifies the proportion of successful episodes that are also collision-free, controlling for task-completion rate.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{SSR and SSR/TSR ratio across conditions. The ratio increase from 0.39 (baseline) to 0.51--0.60 (fine-tuned) indicates that CAR gains reflect learned safer execution, not reduced task exposure.}
\label{tab:ssr}
\vspace{2pt}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Condition & TSR (\%) & CAR (\%) & SSR (\%) & SSR/TSR \\
\midrule
Pi0.5 baseline & 71.5 & 28.5 & 28.0 & 0.39 \\
LoRA-only (3 seeds) & 51.5 & 54.67 & 27.7 & 0.54 \\
SDF $\lambda{=}0.005$ (3 seeds) & 54.0 & 54.5 & 27.8 & 0.51 \\
SDF $\lambda{=}0.01$ (3 seeds) & 53.8 & 56.33 & 30.2 & 0.56 \\
SDF $\lambda{=}0.1$ (3 seeds) & 50.2 & 57.00 & 30.2 & 0.60 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\section{Per-Task Oracle Analysis}
\label{sec:appendix_pertask}
\label{sec:appendix_pertask_oracle}

Table~\ref{tab:oracle_pertask} shows per-task CAR for oracle refinement. On task~1 (\texttt{chocolate\_pudding}), where the EE is the primary collision body and the LoRA baseline achieves only 46.0\% CAR, Oracle-adaptive improves to 55.0\% and Oracle-feature to 47.5\%. On the remaining tasks, all oracle variants degrade: Oracle-adaptive reduces task~0 (\texttt{orange\_juice}) from 96.0\% to 82.5\% and task~2 (\texttt{milk}) from 98.0\% to 77.5\%. The SDF gradient pushes the EE away from the nearest surface, but the resulting joint-space displacement rotates intermediate arm links into obstacle volumes.

The feature-space oracle fires on nearly every timestep (241.7 refinements/episode vs.\ 14.2 for Cartesian variants) but with gradient magnitudes two orders of magnitude smaller (0.004--0.006 vs.\ 0.18--0.49), and CAR (76.9\%) shows no advantage despite 17$\times$ more steps. The A3 learned SDF never triggers refinement (0 steps/episode), as its outputs avoid the activation threshold.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Per-task CAR (\%) for oracle SDF refinement variants. EE-only refinement helps only on task~1 where the EE is the primary collision body; on other tasks it trades one collision site for another.}
\label{tab:oracle_pertask}
\vspace{2pt}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
Method & Task 0 & Task 1 & Task 2 & Task 3 & Agg. \\
\midrule
LoRA baseline & 96.0 & 46.0 & 98.0 & 88.0 & 82.0 \\
\midrule
Oracle-adaptive & 82.5 & 55.0 & 77.5 & 85.0 & 75.0 \\
Oracle-fixed & 85.0 & 50.0 & 77.5 & 80.0 & 73.1 \\
Oracle-feature & 90.0 & 47.5 & 82.5 & 87.5 & 76.9 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}


\section{Null Distribution for SDF Loss Weight Analysis}
\label{sec:appendix_null}

We construct a null distribution by pooling the three LoRA-only seeds and three $\lambda = 0.005$ seeds into six per-seed CAR measurements: 56.5\% (LoRA s0, re-evaluated), 52.5\% (LoRA s1), 55.0\% (LoRA s2), 57.0\% ($\lambda{=}0.005$ s0), 54.0\% ($\lambda{=}0.005$ s1), 52.5\% ($\lambda{=}0.005$ s2), yielding mean 54.58\% and standard deviation 1.93\% (Bessel-corrected, $n{-}1 = 5$). We treat these as exchangeable because SDF at $\lambda = 0.005$ produces no separation from LoRA-only. The $\lambda = 0.01$ mean of 56.33\% falls within $0.8\sigma$ of this null ($p > 0.1$), consistent with seed-to-seed variation rather than a treatment effect. The $\lambda = 0.1$ 30K mean of 57.00\% (3 seeds: 58.0\%, 58.5\%, 54.5\%) yields a two-tailed permutation $p = 0.131$ (one-tailed $p = 0.071$). The result is non-significant at conventional thresholds ($\alpha = 0.05$).

The permutation test makes no distributional assumptions. Pooling the six null values $\{56.5, 52.5, 55, 57, 54, 52.5\}$ and three $\lambda = 0.1$ values $\{58, 58.5, 54.5\}$ into nine measurements, we enumerate all $\binom{9}{3} = 84$ three-element combinations and compute the fraction whose mean $\geq 57.00$. Six combinations meet this threshold, yielding an exact one-tailed permutation $p = 6/84 \approx 0.071$. For the two-tailed test, we additionally count combinations on the lower tail with equal or greater distance from the pooled mean, yielding $p = 11/84 \approx 0.131$. With the third seed ($s_2 = 54.5\%$) pulling the $\lambda = 0.1$ mean from 58.25\% (2 seeds) down to 57.00\%, the marginal signal observed at $n = 2$ dissolves into seed-to-seed variance.

A Phase~2 ablation reinforces this conclusion. Setting $\lambda = 0$ during Phase~2 (steps 5K--30K), so that SDF supervision is active only during auxiliary head warmup (Phase~1, steps 0--5K), yields CAR of 57.5\% (single seed, s2). This value lies within the null range, indicating that SDF training produces no lasting behavioral effect on collision avoidance.

\section{Step-wise Checkpoint Analysis for \texorpdfstring{$\lambda = 0.1$}{lambda = 0.1}}
\label{sec:appendix_stepwise}

Table~\ref{tab:stepwise_lambda01} reports per-seed CAR at seven checkpoints for $\lambda = 0.1$. Seeds~0 and~1 are available at 16K, 20K, 22K, and 30K; 24K--28K report seed-0 only; seed~2 is available at 30K only. The seed-0 curve shows no monotonic trend through 28K. At 30K, the addition of seed~2 (54.5\%) pulls the three-seed mean to 57.00\%, within the null band ($p = 0.131$).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Per-seed CAR (\%) at training checkpoints for $\lambda = 0.1$ (200 episodes each). 24K--28K: seed-0 only. The 30K checkpoint includes all three seeds; the three-seed mean (57.00\%) remains within the null band ($p = 0.131$, two-tailed permutation).}
\label{tab:stepwise_lambda01}
\vspace{2pt}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Checkpoint & Seed 0 & Seed 1 & Seed 2 & Mean \\
\midrule
16K steps & 55.5 & 56.0 & -- & 55.75 \\
20K steps & 55.0 & 56.0 & -- & 55.5 \\
22K steps & 60.5 & 56.5 & -- & 58.5 \\
24K steps & 52.5 & -- & -- & -- \\
26K steps & 53.5 & -- & -- & -- \\
28K steps & 54.5 & -- & -- & -- \\
30K steps & 58.0 & 58.5 & 54.5 & 57.00 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Individual seeds exhibit checkpoint-to-checkpoint CAR fluctuations of up to 8\,pp (seed~0: 52.5--60.5\%), larger than the cross-seed standard deviation at any fixed checkpoint ($\leq$3\,pp). This is consistent with binomial sampling noise at $n = 50$ episodes per task ($\sigma \approx 7$\,pp per task for $p \approx 0.5$). At 30K, the third seed ($s_2 = 54.5\%$) falls well within the null range, pulling the three-seed mean to 57.00\% and rendering the earlier two-seed departure non-significant ($p = 0.131$). The seed-averaged curve shows no monotonic trend, confirming that SDF loss weight does not produce a progressive training signal.


\section{Oracle SDF Refinement Details}
\label{sec:appendix_oracle}

All oracle variants replace the learned SDF decoder with ground-truth distance fields computed from the simulation geometry. Each is evaluated for 160 episodes (4 tasks $\times$ 40).

\paragraph{Oracle-adaptive.} At each timestep, if the ground-truth SDF at the end-effector position falls below a safety margin, the action is adjusted by a step in the negative SDF gradient direction. The step size $\alpha$ is scaled inversely with distance: smaller distances produce larger corrections. This mimics an adaptive potential field controller.

\paragraph{Oracle-fixed.} Identical to Oracle-adaptive except the step size is fixed at $\alpha = 0.005$ regardless of distance. This isolates the effect of step-size adaptation from the refinement geometry.

\paragraph{Oracle-feature.} Instead of modifying the action directly in Cartesian space, this variant applies the SDF gradient in the policy's latent feature space via backpropagation. The gradient of the ground-truth SDF value with respect to the intermediate features is computed and used to perturb the features before the action head decodes them. This tests whether geometric corrections are more effective when applied in the model's learned representation space. In practice, the feature-space variant fires on nearly every timestep (241.7 refinements per episode vs.\ 14.2 for Cartesian variants) but with gradient magnitudes two orders of magnitude smaller (0.004--0.006 vs.\ 0.18--0.49 in action space).


\section{Cross-Scene Validation: spatial\_bowl}
\label{sec:appendix_spatial_bowl}

To test whether the SDF redundancy finding generalizes beyond the primary \texttt{object\_pudding} scene, we replicate the core comparison on \texttt{spatial\_bowl}, a separate SafeLIBERO suite with a distinct obstacle layout (moka pot, wine bottle, milk carton, red coffee mug, yellow book). Both conditions use the same architecture, hyperparameters, and two-phase training schedule (30K steps), differing only in the training scene.

Table~\ref{tab:spatial_bowl} reports the results. The SDF condition ($\lambda = 0.005$) yields CAR of 11.5\% versus 15.5\% for LoRA-only, a $-$4.0\,pp difference indicating that SDF supervision is not only redundant but slightly detrimental in this scene. TSR is comparable across conditions (50.0\% vs.\ 51.5\%). Both evaluations use a single training seed (seed~0) and a single evaluation seed (seed~7, 200 episodes), so statistical power is limited; nevertheless, the qualitative pattern---SDF failing to improve over LoRA-only---is consistent with the \texttt{object\_pudding} findings.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Cross-scene validation on \texttt{spatial\_bowl} (single training seed, 200 episodes, 30K checkpoint). The $-$4.0\,pp CAR difference indicates SDF is not only redundant but slightly detrimental, consistent with \texttt{object\_pudding}.}
\label{tab:spatial_bowl}
\vspace{2pt}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
Condition & CAR (\%) & TSR (\%) \\
\midrule
LoRA-only & 15.5 & 50.0 \\
